(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-04"] = {"chem-12-04-m01":{"idea":"Systems are named by what they can exchange with the surroundings: matter, energy, both or neither.","steps":["The lid is closed, so no matter can leave.","A thermos is insulated, so ideally no heat passes either.","A system that exchanges neither matter nor energy is an isolated system.","The thermos is the ideal example."],"others":"An open system exchanges both. A system that exchanges matter but not energy is not a standard type. A closed system exchanges energy but not matter, but a thermos is built to stop heat as well.","answer":"a system that cannot exchange matter and, as an ideal case, no energy either"},"chem-12-04-m02":{"idea":"State functions describe where the system is, not how it got there.","steps":["A state function depends only on the present state.","Examples are U, H, S and G.","The route taken between two states does not change its change.","Heat q and work w depend on the route, so they are not state functions."],"others":"Heat exchanged, the path and time taken are all linked to how the change happens, so they are not state function properties.","answer":"only on the present state of the system, and not on how it reached that state"},"chem-12-04-m03":{"idea":"Intensive properties do not depend on how much matter you have.","steps":["Take the system and cut it in half.","Temperature stays the same.","Mass, volume and internal energy halve.","So temperature is intensive; the others are extensive."],"others":"Mass, volume and internal energy all double when the amount doubles, so they are extensive.","trap":"Intensive: T, P, density. Extensive: m, V, U, H.","answer":"temperature"},"chem-12-04-m04":{"idea":"The first law is energy conservation. The sign convention matters.","steps":["Work done on the system is positive.","Heat absorbed by the system is positive.","Both increase the internal energy.","So ΔU = q + w."],"others":"ΔU = w − q and ΔU = q − w subtract one of the terms, which does not match this convention (q − w is for work done by the system). ΔU = q/w is not an energy balance.","trap":"With work done BY the system, the law is ΔU = q − w.","answer":"ΔU = q + w"},"chem-12-04-m05":{"idea":"Work in expansion against constant pressure is P_ext times the volume change, with a sign.","steps":["The gas pushes the surroundings back, so it loses energy as work.","w = −P_ext × ΔV.","ΔV = V₂ − V₁.","So w = −P_ext(V₂ − V₁), which is negative."],"others":"A positive sign would mean work is done on the gas, but expansion is the gas doing work. The forms that divide by ΔV or by P_ext have wrong units for work.","answer":"−P_ext(V₂ − V₁)"},"chem-12-04-m06":{"idea":"Work against zero pressure is zero.","steps":["w = −P_ext ΔV.","In a vacuum P_ext = 0.","So w = 0, even though the volume increases."],"others":"The sign does not depend on whether volume increases; it depends on P_ext. The idea −PV uses the gas pressure, but the surroundings do not push back. A positive value would need work done on the gas.","answer":"zero, because the external pressure is zero"},"chem-12-04-m07":{"idea":"For an ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature.","steps":["Isothermal means T is constant.","So ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas.","ΔU = q + w = 0 gives q = −w.","The gas does work on the surroundings, and takes in an equal amount of heat."],"others":"q = 0 describes an adiabatic change. ΔU positive or negative would need a temperature change.","answer":"ΔU = 0, so the heat absorbed equals the work done by the gas"},"chem-12-04-m08":{"idea":"Adiabatic means no heat flows in or out.","steps":["Adiabatic: q = 0.","ΔU = q + w = 0 + w.","So ΔU = w.","If the gas expands, it does work, ΔU is negative and it cools."],"others":"ΔT = 0 is for an isothermal change. w = 0 would not be a feature of an adiabatic process. ΔU = 0 with q = −w describes an isothermal ideal gas.","answer":"q = 0, so ΔU = w"},"chem-12-04-m09":{"idea":"Enthalpy adds the pressure-volume term to internal energy.","steps":["H = U + PV.","PV is the energy used to make space for the system.","At constant pressure, q_p = ΔH."],"others":"P/V and U/PV have wrong units for energy. H = U − PV has the wrong sign.","answer":"H = U + PV"},"chem-12-04-m10":{"idea":"Link ΔH and ΔU through the change in moles of gas.","steps":["H = U + PV, so ΔH = ΔU + Δ(PV).","For gases at constant T and P, Δ(PV) = Δn_g RT.","So ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT.","Δn_g = moles of gaseous products − moles of gaseous reactants."],"others":"Subtracting the term has the wrong sign. RT/Δn_g inverts the term. Multiplying ΔU by Δn_g RT is not a sum of energies.","answer":"ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT"},"chem-12-04-m11":{"idea":"ΔH = ΔU only if Δn_g = 0.","steps":["ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT.","Count gas moles: H₂ + I₂ → 2HI has 2 mol on each side.","Δn_g = 2 − 2 = 0, so ΔH = ΔU.","The others change the gas moles: −1, +1 and −2."],"others":"2SO₂ + O₂ → 2SO₃ has Δn_g = −1. PCl₅ → PCl₃ + Cl₂ has Δn_g = +1. N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ has Δn_g = −2.","answer":"H₂(g) + I₂(g) → 2HI(g)"},"chem-12-04-m12":{"idea":"Exothermic means heat leaves the system.","steps":["The system gives out heat.","H_products is lower than H_reactants.","ΔH = H_products − H_reactants, so it is negative."],"others":"Positive ΔH is for endothermic reactions. A constant temperature does not make ΔH zero. Giving out heat with a positive sign is a contradiction.","trap":"Exo = exit = negative.","answer":"negative, because the system gives out heat"},"chem-12-04-m13":{"idea":"Formation enthalpy is the heat to make a compound from its elements.","steps":["Forming an element from itself needs no change.","So Δ_fH° = 0 for an element in its most stable form.","Examples: O₂(g), C(graphite), H₂(g).","Diamond and ozone are not the most stable forms, so they are not zero."],"others":"It is not equal to combustion or atomisation enthalpy, which are not zero. It is not always positive.","answer":"zero"},"chem-12-04-m14":{"idea":"Hess's law uses the fact that H is a state function.","steps":["ΔH depends only on initial and final states.","The path does not matter.","So the total ΔH is the same in one step or in several steps."],"others":"ΔH does not depend on the number of steps, the catalyst or the speed. A catalyst changes the rate but not ΔH.","answer":"the total enthalpy change of a reaction is the same whether it takes place in one step or in several steps"},"chem-12-04-m15":{"idea":"Combustion is burning in oxygen.","steps":["Burning gives out heat.","So the enthalpy change is always negative.","Example: methane, −890 kJ/mol."],"others":"Combustion enthalpy is not zero for all elements, since carbon and hydrogen burn. It is not positive, because burning releases heat. It is not positive for hydrocarbons.","answer":"always negative, as burning in oxygen gives out heat"},"chem-12-04-m16":{"idea":"Strong acid plus strong base always gives the same net reaction.","steps":["Both are fully ionised in dilute solution.","The net reaction is H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O.","The heat is about −57 kJ per mole of water formed, whatever the acid and base."],"others":"−285 kJ is the formation enthalpy of water. Positive 57 would be endothermic, but neutralisation gives out heat. The temperature does rise, so zero is wrong.","answer":"−57 kJ per mole of water formed"},"chem-12-04-m17":{"idea":"Use Hess's law with the steps solid → liquid → gas.","steps":["Sublimation is solid → gas.","The same change can go solid → liquid (fusion) and liquid → gas (vaporisation).","So Δ_subH = Δ_fusH + Δ_vapH."],"others":"The other forms put fusion or vaporisation on the wrong side or subtract. Sublimation is the largest of the three.","answer":"Δ_subH = Δ_fusH + Δ_vapH"},"chem-12-04-m18":{"idea":"Entropy is about how many ways a system can be arranged.","steps":["More ways to arrange particles and energy means more disorder.","Entropy measures this disorder or randomness.","It rises when a solid melts or a gas expands."],"others":"Work capacity, total heat content and total energy are different ideas, linked to free energy, enthalpy and internal energy.","answer":"the disorder or randomness of a system"},"chem-12-04-m19":{"idea":"Entropy falls when particles become more ordered.","steps":["Water vapour has molecules moving freely.","In liquid water they are closer together and less free.","The system becomes more ordered, so entropy decreases."],"others":"A gas expanding, ice melting and a solid dissolving into ions all increase disorder, so entropy rises in each.","trap":"Gas → liquid → solid: entropy falls.","answer":"water vapour condenses to liquid water"},"chem-12-04-m20":{"idea":"Spontaneity depends on total entropy.","steps":["The second law: for a spontaneous process, ΔS_total > 0.","ΔS_total = ΔS_system + ΔS_surroundings.","Neither ΔH alone nor ΔS of the system alone decides it."],"others":"Exothermic does not guarantee spontaneity. The enthalpy does not always increase. A system entropy decrease with no change in surroundings gives a lower total entropy.","answer":"the total entropy of the system and its surroundings increases"},"chem-12-04-m21":{"idea":"Gibbs energy combines enthalpy and entropy.","steps":["ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","T is in kelvin.","If ΔG is negative, the process is spontaneous."],"others":"Using + TΔS has the wrong sign. The forms with TΔH or ΔS − TΔH do not have the right units.","answer":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS"},"chem-12-04-m22":{"idea":"The sign of ΔG tells the direction.","steps":["ΔG negative: forward reaction is spontaneous.","ΔG positive: reverse reaction is spontaneous.","ΔG = 0: no net tendency either way, so the system is at equilibrium."],"others":"Spontaneous forward or reverse needs ΔG to be negative or positive. ΔG = 0 says nothing about the rate being zero.","answer":"the system is at equilibrium"},"chem-12-04-m23":{"idea":"Check the sign of ΔG = ΔH − TΔS with both ΔH and ΔS positive.","steps":["ΔH > 0 pushes ΔG up, which is unfavourable.","ΔS > 0 makes −TΔS negative, and it grows with T.","At high T, TΔS is larger than ΔH, so ΔG is negative.","So it is spontaneous at high temperature."],"others":"It is not spontaneous at all temperatures, since low T makes ΔH win. Low temperature fails. It does occur at some temperature, so no temperature is wrong.","answer":"at high temperature, when TΔS is larger than ΔH"},"chem-12-04-m24":{"idea":"State functions: U, H, S, G. Not state functions: q and w.","steps":["Internal energy, entropy and enthalpy depend only on the state.","Work depends on the route, for example reversible or irreversible.","So work is not a state function."],"others":"Internal energy, entropy and enthalpy are all state functions.","trap":"Read NOT carefully.","answer":"work done by the system"},"chem-12-04-m25":{"idea":"Open cup means constant pressure. Closed bomb means constant volume.","steps":["A coffee cup is open to the air, so pressure is constant. The heat is ΔH.","A bomb calorimeter is sealed with fixed volume. No PV work, so the heat is ΔU."],"others":"The pair in the reverse order swaps the two devices. Constant temperature is not what the cup measures. They do not both work at constant pressure.","answer":"constant pressure (ΔH) and constant volume (ΔU)"},"chem-12-04-m26":{"idea":"The third law fixes the zero of entropy.","steps":["At 0 K a perfect crystal has only one possible arrangement.","One arrangement means no disorder.","So the entropy is zero."],"others":"Entropy is not equal to enthalpy of fusion. It is not negative, and it is not large.","answer":"zero"},"chem-12-04-m27":{"idea":"Melting at the melting point is an equilibrium.","steps":["Ice becomes water, which is more disordered, so ΔS is positive.","At 0 °C and 1 atm, ice and water are in equilibrium.","At equilibrium ΔG = 0."],"others":"ΔS is not negative or zero for melting. If ΔG were positive, melting would not happen at all.","answer":"ΔS is positive and ΔG = 0"},"chem-12-04-n01":{"idea":"Use the first law: ΔU = q + w, with signs from the system's point of view.","steps":["Heat absorbed: q = +150 J.","Work done by the gas: w = −60 J.","ΔU = 150 + (−60) = 90 J."],"trap":"Work done BY the gas is negative in this convention.","answer":"90 J"},"chem-12-04-n02":{"idea":"Work of expansion against a constant pressure is P_ext times ΔV.","steps":["ΔV = 3.0 − 1.0 = 2.0 L = 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³.","w = −P_ext ΔV = −2.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻³.","w = −400 J, so the magnitude is 400 J."],"trap":"Convert litres to m³ with 1 L = 10⁻³ m³ when pressure is in pascal.","answer":"400 J"},"chem-12-04-n03":{"idea":"Use ΔH = ΔU + Δn_g RT. Count gas moles.","steps":["Δn_g = 2 − (1 + 3) = −2.","RT = 8.314 × 10⁻³ kJ/mol K × 300 = 2.494 kJ/mol.","Δn_g RT = −2 × 2.494 = −4.99 kJ.","ΔH = −91.0 − 4.99 = −96.0 kJ."],"trap":"Put R in kJ to match ΔU in kJ.","answer":"-96.0 kJ"},"chem-12-04-n04":{"idea":"Use Hess's law. Combine the two given equations to get the target.","steps":["Equation 1: C + O₂ → CO₂, ΔH = −393.5 kJ.","Equation 2 reversed: CO₂ → CO + ½O₂, ΔH = +283.0 kJ.","Add them: C + ½O₂ → CO.","ΔH = −393.5 + 283.0 = −110.5 kJ."],"trap":"Reversing an equation changes the sign of ΔH.","answer":"-110.5 kJ"},"chem-12-04-n05":{"idea":"Heat needed = mass × specific heat × temperature change.","steps":["ΔT = 75 − 25 = 50 K.","q = mcΔT = 200 × 4.18 × 50.","q = 41 800 J.","In kJ: 41.8 kJ."],"trap":"A change of 1 °C equals a change of 1 K.","answer":"41.8 kJ"},"chem-12-04-n06":{"idea":"Use ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. Match the units of ΔH and ΔS first.","steps":["ΔS = −100 J/K = −0.100 kJ/K.","TΔS = 300 × (−0.100) = −30 kJ.","ΔG = −40 − (−30) = −10 kJ.","ΔG is negative, so it is spontaneous at 300 K."],"trap":"Do not subtract J from kJ without converting.","answer":"-10.0 kJ"},"chem-12-04-n07":{"idea":"The reaction becomes spontaneous when ΔG turns negative. The boundary is ΔG = 0.","steps":["ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 0 gives T = ΔH/ΔS.","ΔH = 30 kJ = 30 000 J.","T = 30 000 / 100 = 300 K.","Above 300 K, TΔS is larger than ΔH, so ΔG is negative."],"trap":"Both ΔH and ΔS are positive, so high temperature favours the reaction.","answer":"300 K"},"chem-12-04-n08":{"idea":"ΔH = bond energy broken − bond energy formed.","steps":["Bonds broken: 1 H–H and 1 Cl–Cl = 436 + 242 = 678 kJ.","Bonds formed: 2 H–Cl = 2 × 431 = 862 kJ.","ΔH = 678 − 862.","ΔH = −184 kJ."],"trap":"Breaking bonds needs energy; forming bonds gives it out.","answer":"-184 kJ"},"chem-12-04-n09":{"idea":"Find moles burned, then multiply by the heat per mole.","steps":["Moles of CH₄ = 8.0 / 16 = 0.5 mol.","Heat released = 0.5 × 890 kJ.","Heat released = 445 kJ."],"trap":"Half a mole gives half the molar heat.","answer":"445 kJ"},"chem-12-04-n10":{"idea":"At the melting point, solid and liquid are in equilibrium, so ΔG = 0.","steps":["ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 0, so ΔS = ΔH / T.","ΔS = 6010 / 273.","ΔS = 22.0 J/(mol K)."],"trap":"Use kelvin for T and keep ΔH in J.","answer":"22.0 J/(mol K)"}};
